<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edmund Barton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edmund Barton was an Australian lawyer and politician who led the federation movement in the 1890s. He became the first prime minister of the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1901, when Parliament met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, and his government passed the Immigration Restriction Act. In 1903 he left office to become a founding justice of the High Court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-barton.jpg" alt="Edmund Barton" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Barton,_Q.C.,_M.L.C.,_ca._1889_-_unknown_photographer_(5932628140).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edmund Barton was born on 18 January 1849 in Glebe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, the eleventh of twelve children of English parents, William and Mary Louisa Barton, who had arrived in New South Wales in 1827. His father worked as an accountant, stockbroker and estate agent and was once bankrupt; his mother ran a boarding school for girls in hard times. Barton attended Sydney Grammar School, where he was dux and school captain in 1863 and 1864, and matriculated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-sydney">University of Sydney</a> in 1865. He was a member of the Sydney University Cricket Club and a founder of the Sydney Rowing Club, and married Jane Mason Ross in 1877.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After losing a university seat in 1876, Barton won a by-election to the New South Wales Legislative Assembly in 1879. In 1882, aged 33, he became Speaker, the youngest presiding officer of any Australian legislative chamber. He entered the Legislative Council in 1887 and served as Attorney General in 1889. An early supporter of federation, he was a delegate to the National Australasian Convention of March 1891 and helped to produce a draft constitution substantially similar to the one enacted in 1900. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-parkes">Henry Parkes</a>'s government in October 1891, Parkes persuaded him to lead the federal movement in New South Wales.</p><p>In 1897 Barton topped the poll of New South Wales delegates to the Constitutional Convention. Sir Samuel Griffith wrote most of the text of the constitution, but Barton was the political leader who carried it through, and he led the campaign for a yes vote in the referendum. He became prime minister on 1 January 1901, the day federation took effect. His ministry was described as a 'cabinet of kings', and after the March 1901 election he stayed in office with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-labor-party">Australian Labor Party</a>. His government set up the Australian Defence Force and the Commonwealth Public Service, introduced nation-wide women's suffrage, and passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immigration-restriction-act-1901">Immigration Restriction Act 1901</a>. In November 1901 Parliament approved Australian control of British New Guinea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Barton resigned on 24 September 1903 and was sworn in to the High Court on 7 October with Samuel Griffith and Richard O'Connor. On the court he joined Griffith in almost every case and delivered only twelve separate judgments in the 44 cases heard in 1904 and 1905. In 1919 he was disappointed to be passed over as Chief Justice on Griffith's retirement. He died of heart failure on 7 January 1920 at Medlow Bath, New South Wales, and was survived by his wife and six children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edmund Barton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Commonwealth was proclaimed in Centennial Park, Sydney, on 1 January 1901, with <b>Edmund Barton</b> of the Protectionist Party as first prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Edmund Barton</b> was sworn in as the first Prime Minister in Sydney's Centennial Park.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Glebe, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in Medlow Bath, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.88,151.18&amp;year=1895" aria-label="Glebe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edmund Barton"><image href="/maps/globes/g150s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5084 5332h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5084 5332h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.88,151.18&amp;year=1895">What was here in 1895? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
